          "logprobs": [],
          "text": "{\"translation_en\":\"Do **I love you**! Do I love you! Why, I adore you, my love!\",\"larger_context_summary\":\"Rodolphe speaks to Emma Bovary during the culmination of his calculated seduction of her, after their horseback ride into the woods. Emma, a married woman seeking the romantic passion missing from her life with Charles, asks for reassurance that Rodolphe loves her. He answers with extravagant emphasis. The exchange helps inaugurate their adulterous affair, but the wider novel makes his ardor suspect: Rodolphe is an experienced, cynical seducer whose language strategically echoes Emma’s romantic expectations, while she invests the relationship with far greater emotional seriousness.\",\"interpretive_commentary\":\"The first “je t’aime” is part of the idiomatic exclamation “Si je t’aime!”, effectively an emphatic answer to Emma’s request for reassurance rather than a conditional statement. Repetition, exclamation, “I adore you,” and the endearment make the declaration intensely delivered, but intensity alone does not make it a reflexive emotional outburst: grammatically and conversationally, Rodolphe is asserting that he loves Emma. Nor does he explicitly promise fidelity, permanence, or future conduct, even though his words have major relational consequences by encouraging her trust and the affair. The wider plot adds dramatic irony without changing that semantic force. Rodolphe may be exaggerating or deceiving Emma, but an insincere assertion remains an assertion; deception concerns whether he believes the avowal, not what the avowal presents as true.\"}"
        }
